Codex Computer Use marketing promises: When performing any Computer Use task, Codex never hijack cursor or foregrounds new apps on the top of the user application.\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Drawback I found:\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
About never-foregrounds-app: in realty, I DOES. Not always, but yes often it does open app in the foreground hiding user\'92s application and even the control shit to the new app.\uc0\u8232 But, in reality (over multiple in hand use cases), sometimes or even often, it does otherwise:\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
About never-hijacks-cursor: It actually did solve cursor hijacking problem. It never takes the user main cursor for clicking or doing any action.\uc0\u8232 BUT, the problem occurs when it breaks its first promise about never making the app foreground. How? Let\'92s say you are typing with your cursor pointing on the writing section and click writing. But, if Codex opens (foreground) another app on top of your app, they you can\'92t be able to even write. First, it will hides your application view; second, your writing input could be inserted in the new Codex opened application. So, it\'92s not hijacking cursor in the general sense but, it takes over user\'92s focus.\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Security concern: All Codex action routes through ChatGPT models. So, user have no option to use Codex Computer Use with local models.\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
How Meridius solving these problems:\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
