Coasty AI:
\f1\b0  Coasty operates primarily on a visual, screenshot-and-action loop. It functions as a computer-use agent that views the application interface through screenshots, reasons about what it sees, and executes native mouse and keyboard inputs (clicks, typing, scrolling).\uc0\u8232 Recently, Coasty AI goes for Cloud Virtual Machine (VM) approach where on every launch it works on a virtual machine with no native that I need to perform action on. This is actually a sharper point for my competitor answer than "hijacks cursor," because "hijacks cursor" isn't even accurate for Coasty anymore (it doesn't have your cursor to hijack). The real gap is: 
\f0\b Coasty gets a fresh, disconnected copy of your accounts on a machine that isn't yours. Meridius uses your actual native apps, already logged in, on your own Mac
\f1\b0 , no re-authentication, no stored token vault, no web-only workaround for apps that only exist as native software.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Drawback I found:\
\ls1\ilvl1
\f2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}
\f1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Cloud VM: simply put, a different computer with all different app sets. So, in general terms, it might not take over the user\'92s main display, but also can\'92t work with use\'92s native applications.\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Screenshot-and-action loop sometimes break for poor UI\'92s. So relying solely on the visuals can make computer use decision unreliable.\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Security concern: As it takes screenshot in every steps, that means you are sending your raw display visual information to external model to train.\
\ls1\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	
\f2 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
